{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of J3, the real checker runner adapter. The head is 33b8f95b963a6e49f02fea412d84ba3cfbb904f7 (tree aab51594), for request c097372e, under builder promise 3bb0ab54. Verdict: APPROVED.\n\nI read the reporting artifact 7283d674 and its note first. Then I read the request, every change from Main 0881c1bc, and the planner's rulings on the evidence-rules questions, to confirm that this head stays out of that slice.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- Authority chain. The planner wrote the request c097372e. The builder wrote the promise 3bb0ab54, the 31 artifacts and the review request a53554ef. The reporting artifact rests on the promise and the request.\n- Base and delta:\n  - The head descends from Main 0881c1bc through 14 commits.\n  - They change exactly the 31 paths named by the 31 artifacts, one artifact per path, all at this head.\n  - The worktree was clean at the head, and its tree is aab51594.\n- Report-only tail. From the repair checkpoint 69bf4188 to the head, only the note and its manifest are added, both as regular 100644 files. From the gate checkpoint f6fc77bd to the head, the only other change is scripts/active-source.test.mjs.\n- Evidence:\n  - The committed manifest has SHA-256 7bbe35e8…, and the index SHA256SUMS has SHA-256 53359d7d…. Both match the request.\n  - All 1,184 files match their recorded size and hash, 308,571,633 bytes in total. None of the 1,320 entries in the directory is writable. All 1,183 SHA256SUMS lines are OK.\n  - Each of the 83 rows in the note's table matches its retained receipt's exit code and the SHA-256 of its retained output. Each receipt's own output list matches too.\n  - Every source-bound run kept its source unchanged. The only runs without a source binding are three host-runtime probes.\n- Documents:\n  - check-doc-links on an export of the head: 731 links, 0 broken.\n  - check-notes 0881c1bc..33b8f95b: 1 advisory warning. The manifest is 321,021 bytes against the 40,960-byte limit.\n  - check-commit-messages: 14 checked, 0 flagged.\n  - `git diff --check`: clean.\n\nI reran no suite.\n\n## The failed gate and the isolated repair\n\nThe ordinary gate ran at f6fc77bd (tree f6f29ab4) and exited 1.\n- Every step before `test` passed, including typecheck. Kernel passed.\n- Node ran all 76 cases, and 75 passed. The one failure is in scripts/active-source.test.mjs: the inventory of files that reach the checkers package now also lists apps/room/lanes/test/support/container-runner.ts.\n- Counting, plain and Native were not reached.\n- The gate remains failed.\n\nThe rule is in docs/testing.md on Main, under \"Follow-up after a gate\". This head does not change it. I assess each condition at the exact head:\n- One test file failed. The repair at 69bf4188 changes only that file: it adds the one exact path to the expected list, and one comment. The assertion is still an exact deepEqual, and the new entry is the test-only bridge, which only the Native manifest scenario imports. No case, deadline or production assertion changed, and the case names at the gate and at the head are the same 11.\n- No production code, configuration, dependency, shared helper or imported code changed after the gate.\n- Nothing imports the file. scripts/test.mjs and scripts/test-changed.mjs run it by path as a test.\n- No tsconfig includes `scripts/`, so no compiler context's inputs changed.\n- The whole owning file passed, 11 of 11 (gate-followup-owning-node-v2). The first follow-up lacked the phase setting and exited 7, and it stays recorded as failed.\n- The unreached stages passed at 69bf4188:\n  - Counting: 55 passed.\n  - plain: 2,930 passed and 1 skipped, in 293 files.\n  - Native: 825 passed and 2 skipped, in 238 files.\n- The note keeps the failed gate and the follow-ups as separate outcomes.\n\nThe repair and its evidence meet the rule's conditions. I accept the follow-up in place of a second gate. This does not make the failed gate a pass.\n\n## The acceptance witness\n\nActual acceptance 6, at f6fc77bd, exited 0 with the source unchanged. It used the local TEST image sha256:aeb2f9a3….\n- 8 direct container jobs. All 8 origins closed, and the Gateway saw 0 updates.\n  - exact tree, custody, network and private write: passed;\n  - real failure: failed;\n  - exact status 17: passed, because that job's configuration declares 17 as the pass status;\n  - missing executable: runner-lost;\n  - hostile PATH and forged output: judgment-unreadable;\n  - wrong tree: checkout-unconfirmed;\n  - fresh filesystem: passed;\n  - bounded termination: limits-passed.\n- One Native proposal scenario with 2 actual runner jobs:\n  - The passing proposal publishes exactly its reservation tree.\n  - The second proposal fails under the same configuration. main stays at the published commit, the lane records `required-check-failed`, and no reservation ref is published.\n  - A signed result for another tree is refused with `not-this-job`, and a delivery for another tree with `not-as-given`.\n\nThese are the three conditions of the request's \"Done when\". Earlier acceptance runs 1 to 5 remain failures.\n\nControls:\n- Four focused controls distinguish: origin join, recovery job identity, claim size, and the setup-error guard.\n- In the setup-guard control, the mutant deletes only the ambiguous-status guard in container-engine.ts. The compiled mutant's actual job then records a completed status-1 step for a missing executable. The witness fails its `complete` against `lost` assertion. The source was restored, and the original file's SHA-256 (0bdb10cb…) is the same as the file at the head.\n- The first claim-size control, which exited 2, stays recorded as inconclusive.\n\n## Architecture\n\n- Package boundary. The new `./node` entry exports `containerRunner` and `gatewayOrigin`. The platform-neutral entry gains only `RunIdentityRefusal` and imports no engine or filesystem owner. No Worker, native definition or deployment changes.\n- Runner contract. `find(run, expectedJob)` compares the recorded job with the expected one before it releases a retained report. After a lost reply, the service looks up the same run once and never calls `run` again. A running record makes the service wait, and no record ends as `run-lost`. The existing judge still decides the outcome: a pass needs the configured step status and line pair.\n- Execution:\n  - A private run directory under a 0700 root holds a claim, the ask, a launch record, the started record, the origin-closed acknowledgement and a terminal record. Each is written owner-only through an exclusive hard link, with a directory sync.\n  - A detached supervisor owns one execution, and it refuses to start twice for the same record.\n  - Reusing a run identifier with a different ask or command prefix is refused.\n- Engine:\n  - nerdctl runs the container. Before any step counts, the adapter checks the image digest against the configuration, and never pulls.\n  - It also checks the ordered environment, the user, the capabilities, seccomp, the namespaces, the loopback-only network, the allowed mounts, and the memory, process and CPU limits in both the metadata and the cgroup files.\n  - Steps run through a separate ctr tasks exec prefix, so the exact exit status is kept.\n- Legacy reads go through the relay to the existing read-only Gateway, with `grant.update` required to be null. Snapshot jobs check out their verified reservation objects and open no origin.\n- Documents. docs/architecture.md and docs/testing.md describe the adapter and the explicit command. The package README states the engine, image and custody requirements and the limits.\n\n## Security\n\n- Custody. The signer, the read token and the run records stay on the host.\n  - The relay runs as UID 65533, and job processes run as UID 65532, without capabilities or new privileges.\n  - The job can reach only the relay on 127.0.0.1:18080.\n  - The relay forwards only GET and POST with three request headers. It runs the trusted exec path only for git, id and mkdir, with a fixed environment.\n  - Only the relay's own stdio reaches the host.\n- Judgment. A result counts only from host-observed process status and the last stdout line, matched against the configuration's declared pairs. Report JSON printed by the job is job output, never a report: the forged-output case ends `judgment-unreadable`.\n- Ambiguous results. A negative status, status 255, or a nonzero status with stderr output ends the run as lost. None of them can produce a pass. The control shows this guard matters.\n- At most one execution. Neither recovery nor a lost reply starts a replacement. A lost run is a check-error, and it does not pass a required check.\n- Cleanup comes before eligibility. A report is released only after the container is physically removed and found absent. For legacy reads, it also needs the durable origin-closed acknowledgement. Otherwise the run is lost.\n- Wrong trees are refused, both at checkout and at native admission.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe production TypeScript change is 956 lines added and 22 removed, across 9 files in packages/checkers/src, plus the 203-line relay and a 10-line Dockerfile. That is proportionate to a real isolated runner with durable at-most-once recovery. Test-side changes are kept apart from production: one null-default bridge, a separate config and an explicit command.\n\n## Points that do not block\n\n1. Empty run directory. If the host crashes after `mkdir` of the run directory and before claim.json is written, `find` throws, not null. The service then waits on every later delivery, and the job never ends as `run-lost`. A corrupt record behaves the same way. Safety holds, because no second execution starts. But the note's open limit says such a crash \"can leave `run-lost`\", and that is true only for a crash before the `mkdir`. A later slice could treat a directory with no claim, older than the grace period, as no record. Until then, an operator has to clear it.\n2. Naming. A claim over 16 KiB is refused with `RunIdentityRefusal`, so the service logs `identity-refused`. The outcome is correct (no execution, `run-lost`), but the name describes a different refusal.\n3. Stderr profile. With the supported ctr profile, a real test command that fails and writes to stderr, as most test runners do, ends as `run-lost`, not `failed`. It still blocks publication. The README and the open limits say that configurations should merge their streams. A failing check's reason is clearer that way.\n4. Manifest size. The manifest is 321,021 bytes, about eight times the advisory limit of 40,960. The note does not mention the warning. It is advisory only, and the 1,184-file inventory explains it.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved.\n  - Custody stays outside the job.\n  - Only declared status and line pairs can pass.\n  - Ambiguous and forged results never pass.\n  - There is at most one execution per record.\n  - Cleanup and origin closure come before a report counts.\n- Simplification: approved, with the naming point noted.\n- Request conditions:\n  - a real check ran on a proposal's exact tree;\n  - a failing check blocked the publication;\n  - results for another tree were refused;\n  - the capacity budgets were judged earlier (approval 664f5611, landed 62423347);\n  - there is no Worker change;\n  - the gate condition is met through the docs/testing.md isolated-test follow-up, as assessed above, with the original gate kept as failed.\n\nThis approval covers the software head only. It does not cover a hosted checker service, provider custody, a production image or registry attestation, guest CPU, or any deployment. The evidence-rules first slice a53e1464 stays separate. The landing check will follow the landing.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:7283d674dbcc033b9a85f33fa1fcdbaca950c852","binding":"assigned","head":"33b8f95b963a6e49f02fea412d84ba3cfbb904f7","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:06706b0717cd09fe0ba5d66ddbe017739ac3845d\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:3bb0ab5446a9a7c19962216d4c7f90736714bae4\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:06706b0717cd09fe0ba5d66ddbe017739ac3845d","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}